-> 👉 **Note**<br>
-> Tracking modifiers do not accept pairs directly such as `Changed(ChildOf(parent))`. Instead, pass the base relation to the modifier and add the pair as a separate query parameter to filter by target.
+Tracking modifiers also accept **relation pairs** to track changes for a specific target:

 ```js
 const parent = world.spawn()

-// Filter changed entities by a specific target
-const changedChildren = world.query(Changed(ChildOf), ChildOf(parent))
+// Track when entities add a ChildOf relation to this specific parent
+const newChildren = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track when entities remove a ChildOf relation to this specific parent
+const orphaned = world.query(Removed(ChildOf(parent)))
+
+// Track when relation data changes for this specific parent
+const updated = world.query(Changed(ChildOf(parent)))
+```
+
+Pair tracking is per target. Adding a second target and removing a non-last target are detected the same as first and last ones. Replacing the target of an exclusive relation produces both a removal and an addition, and destroying an entity fires pair-level removals for all of its active pairs. Within a single observation window, opposite pair events on the same target cancel each other out.
+
+The target `'*'` acts as a wildcard and fires for any target:
+
+```js
+// Fires when an entity adds a ChildOf relation to any target
+const anyNewChildren = world.query(Added(ChildOf('*')))
+```
+
+Pairs can be combined with regular traits in the same query — all constraints are satisfied together:
+
+```js
+// Only entities that added the relation to this parent AND have a Position
+const positionedChildren = world.query(Added(ChildOf(parent)), Position)
 ```

